---
import '../../styles/global.css';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';

const articles = [
  { label: 'Article 01 · LitBuy spreadsheet', title: 'LitBuy Spreadsheet: How to Research Product Links Before You Order', description: 'A practical, fact-led guide for the search intent behind “litbuy spreadsheet”.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: litbuy spreadsheet · Related: LitBuy product finds, LitBuy product links, Taobao agent, Weidian agent, 1688 agent', sections: [
    ['Start with the real job behind the search', `People searching for a LitBuy spreadsheet are usually not looking for a promise. They are trying to turn a scattered product search into a repeatable research process. A product may be on Taobao, Weidian or 1688; its title can be abbreviated, its price shown in CNY, and its photos may not answer every question. A spreadsheet-style catalogue can organise visible records so a visitor can compare titles, categories, product IDs, source prices and media before opening an order route. It cannot guarantee stock, seller performance, authenticity, final condition, customs treatment, shipping cost or delivery time.

LitBuy’s public order guidance says that buyers can copy product links from Taobao, Weidian and 1688 into its search bar for order processing. That is the useful factual boundary. Use a catalogue for discovery and comparison, then use the live LitBuy item and order flow to confirm the exact option, current price and current terms. A saved row is not a checkout receipt, and a helpful product find is not a permanent recommendation.`],
    ['Make the query specific enough to check', `A broad term such as “LitBuy spreadsheet” can hide several tasks. One buyer wants shoes, another is trying to re-find a numerical Weidian ID, another wants a CNY-to-USD comparison, and another wants visible media before opening a link. Search works better when the task is explicit. Combine the product name with a category, the visible product ID, or a marketplace term such as “Weidian link” or “1688 link.” This makes it less likely that a generic title will be mistaken for an exact product.

Inside this site, start with All Products when the category is unknown, then use category and title search to narrow the result. Retain the ID alongside the title. Names can change and several listings can use similar wording, whereas an ID gives you a clearer reference for returning to the same exported record. The ID is not proof of quality; it is simply a practical research handle.`],
    ['Read a product card in the safest order', `First read the category and title. They show how the source record has been grouped, not whether it is available or suitable. Next retain the product ID and original CNY amount. The displayed USD amount is a browsing estimate, not a final payable quote. Options, service charges, shipping, taxes and payment conversion can change what is due later. Keep CNY as the source reference and reconfirm the total in the live LitBuy flow.

Then inspect the main image and any source-media entry. Images can show visible details such as colour, silhouette, obvious hardware, a label or a size-chart image. They cannot show future inventory, prove the seller will dispatch the same version, establish fit, or settle a quality claim. The sensible result of image review is a clearer question to check later, not certainty.`],
    ['Compare like with like', `The lowest visible source price is not always the lowest price for the same item. One listing may refer to a deposit, an accessory, a single component, a different colour or a different size. Before comparing records, check the live option names, included pieces, size, material and version. A price comparison only helps when the products are genuinely comparable.

Create a short note for each candidate: product ID, title, selected option, source price and the date checked. This prevents the common mistake of reopening a similar-looking listing and assuming it was the one you researched. If a source record has disappeared or the live details differ, do not guess why. Treat that as a signal to reassess the current listing.`],
    ['Use product finds as a shortlist, not a seller scorecard', `A directory can make discovery faster because it groups many items and categories in one place. It should not label a seller “verified”, claim an item is genuine, or predict future product quality. Sellers may change listing text, inventory, price and images. The useful division of work is simple: the catalogue helps build a shortlist; the live listing supports the current decision.

This also means “no decision” is a valid result. If the live variant, image or price does not match what you expected, return to the internal catalogue and compare another record. A spreadsheet should reduce wasted searching, not create pressure to buy the first item that looks familiar.`],
    ['A careful first-order workflow', `First, search the internal catalogue and save only a small shortlist. Second, compare title, ID, main image and CNY source price on the internal detail page. Third, open the final LitBuy route only when ready to inspect the live item and its options. Fourth, record the size, colour, version and quantity you actually want. Fifth, when an order reaches a warehouse stage, review the current order information and any available inspection photos before deciding about international shipping.

LitBuy’s public home and help materials describe quality checks, inspection photos, storage and the ability to ship orders together. These are reasons to review each stage carefully, not reasons to skip confirmation. Policies and availability can change with the order, item and destination, so the live order and official help information remain the decision source.`],
    ['What a useful LitBuy spreadsheet should do', `A useful LitBuy spreadsheet is an organised discovery layer. It should allow category browsing, product-ID search, a transparent CNY reference, a clearly labelled USD estimate and an internal detail page before the visitor chooses a final route. It should not invent QC evidence, rewrite marketplace claims as facts, or promise an international-shipping outcome.

That approach answers the search intent honestly. Begin with a specific query, compare the visible facts, confirm the live product and order information, and treat each final payment or parcel decision as a fresh check. That is slower than a blanket claim, but it is far more useful for a buyer who wants to make a traceable choice.`],
    ['Keep a research record that survives a changed listing', `A practical research record contains the product ID, the category, the exact visible title, the CNY source amount, the option you intend to select, the date checked and a short note about what the image did or did not show. This is deliberately ordinary information. It does not attempt to judge the seller or predict an outcome. Its value is that it lets you identify a mismatch before money is committed.

When you return to the live LitBuy route, compare that record with the current listing one field at a time. If the colour, price, option or title differs, stop and decide based on the current information. Do not silently copy an earlier assumption across to a new listing. This habit is especially useful for shoppers comparing several similar products across Taobao, Weidian and 1688. It turns a catalogue from a collection of attractive images into a tool for controlled product research.`]
  ]},
  { label: 'Article 02 · LitBuy QC photos', title: 'LitBuy QC Photos: How to Review Warehouse Images Without Overclaiming What They Prove', description: 'A detailed guide for “LitBuy QC photos” searchers: what inspection media can show and what remains unverified.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: LitBuy QC photos · Related: LitBuy quality check, warehouse inspection photos, QC photo guide, product research', sections: [
    ['QC photos are evidence, not a guarantee', `“LitBuy QC photos” is a high-intent search because product thumbnails rarely answer every question. LitBuy’s public home page and refund material state that products arriving at its warehouse receive a basic quality check and inspection photos. That is useful evidence at a particular moment: it can show visible characteristics of the item at the warehouse. It does not make a photograph a guarantee about authenticity, durability, fit, hidden defects, customs acceptance or final delivery.

The better method is to decide what you need to check before reviewing images. For one item it may be the selected colour and quantity; for another, a visible label, an included component or an obvious mark. A vague review of many pictures can create false confidence. A short list of visible questions makes the decision more useful.`],
    ['Separate reference media from your own order', `A directory can contain source photos or reference media that help with a pre-order comparison. These are not automatically photos of the item in your own order. Warehouse QC photos are different because they relate to an item that has arrived for that order. Keep these evidence types separate in your notes: label catalogue images as reference media and warehouse images as order-specific inspection.

This avoids a frequent error: assuming that a clean reference image predicts a later unit. It cannot. Reference media is best used to identify the details you want to inspect later. The warehouse stage is where you compare the current item with the chosen variant and live listing.`],
    ['Build an item-specific visual checklist', `For clothing, check the visible size label, colour, quantity and obvious external marks. For footwear, note the selected size, the visible pair, colourway and obvious shape. For bags and accessories, check the chosen colour, visible hardware, straps, closure and included pieces. For electronics, confirm the visible model, plug or configuration and obvious external condition; do not infer functionality from an image.

The word “visible” is essential. Lighting can affect colour, perspective can affect proportions, and a missing angle is not evidence that everything is correct. When an image cannot answer a question, use the current order or support path to ask the exact question rather than guessing.`],
    ['Compare with the live order, not memory', `Review order-specific images beside the live order information and your own selection. Check the variant name, size, colour, quantity and the features that mattered when you chose the item. This is safer than relying on an old screenshot because listings can change. Use the product ID and a dated note to maintain a clear trail.

Avoid conclusions such as “perfect”, “best batch” or “verified”. General images cannot establish them. A better note is specific: “The image appears to show the selected blue option and one visible item” or “The supplied views do not show the requested component.” Specific notes are easier to act on and less likely to mislead.`],
    ['When something is unclear', `If a photo appears to show a mismatch, pause before international shipment. Describe the order, option selected and the image that raised the question through the current LitBuy process. Do not assume a refund, exchange or return will be approved. The available remedy can depend on the active policy, seller conditions, item state and timing. LitBuy publishes refund and after-sales information, so review the current notice rather than relying on a generic social post.

The same principle applies to missing images. “No photo visible” is not a quality conclusion; it means there is less evidence. Decide whether you need clarification before moving to the parcel stage.`],
    ['QC and shipping are separate decisions', `A visual check can support an item decision, while shipping requires a different assessment. Parcel choices may depend on destination, line availability, weight, dimensions, contents, insurance choices and restrictions. QC media does not establish any of those outcomes. Keep inspection notes separate from parcel and shipping notes so approval of a visible product detail does not become accidental approval of an international-shipping decision.

LitBuy’s public materials mention storage and combined shipping. These can help a buyer coordinate multiple orders, but do not remove the need to read current route restrictions and order-level options. Before a parcel is submitted, revisit the current policy and destination information.`],
    ['The responsible conclusion', `QC photos are useful because they add visible evidence between purchase and international shipment. Used responsibly, they help compare an arrived item with the selected option, identify questions and make a more informed next decision. They are not a certificate, a universal review of every later unit or a shipping promise.

For a LitBuy QC-photo searcher, the practical workflow is: use catalogue media to form questions, use order-specific inspection images to check visible facts, document any mismatch precisely and consult the live order and policy information before acting. This is the way to obtain a useful answer without turning a photo into an unsupported claim.`],
    ['Document evidence before you escalate a question', `A concise evidence note makes support communication clearer. Include the product ID, the selected option, the order reference available to you, the visible point you are checking and the relevant image angle. For example, state that the visible label does not appear to match the selected size, or that the supplied view does not show an included component. Avoid conclusions the image cannot support.

This record helps separate a visual question from a claim about shipping or seller intent. It also helps you revisit the decision later if several orders arrive together. The aim is not to create an argument; it is to give the current order process enough specific information to address the point. If the live policy identifies a next step, follow that policy rather than a generic QC checklist.`]
  ]},
  { label: 'Article 03 · Shipping and storage', title: 'LitBuy Shipping, Storage and Consolidation: A Research Checklist for International Orders', description: 'A research-first answer to “LitBuy shipping”, “LitBuy storage” and combined-parcel questions.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: LitBuy shipping · Related: LitBuy storage, LitBuy consolidation, international shipping from China, forwarding order, parcel submission', sections: [
    ['Separate the shipping questions', `A search for “LitBuy shipping” often includes several questions: Can items be stored? Can orders ship together? Which route is available? What will a parcel cost? How long might it take? Which items are restricted? LitBuy’s public materials describe international shipping, storage and combined-order features. Those statements explain a workflow, not a universal price or delivery promise. The live parcel flow is where current options must be checked.

Actual cost and timing can depend on destination, line availability, actual and volumetric weight, parcel dimensions, contents, restrictions, carrier events and import procedures. Use a research page to prepare the questions; use the current order and shipping screens to answer them for the particular parcel.`],
    ['Use storage as a planning tool', `LitBuy’s public home page currently advertises storage for up to 120 days and says the first 90 days are free. The Help Center also has storage-period information. Terms and fees can change, so check the live notice and order status before relying on a stated allowance. Operationally, storage can give a buyer time to wait for several purchased items before preparing a parcel.

Use that time actively. List items still expected, items already checked and items with unresolved questions. Do not leave an order unreviewed until a storage deadline. A late discovery about a variant, quantity or visible issue leaves less time to use any current after-sales process.`],
    ['Consolidate from an inventory', `Combined shipping starts with a clear inventory, not a guess. Record each order’s product ID, visible description, quantity, item type and warehouse status. Review any order-specific inspection media for items that arrived. Then decide whether the items belong in one parcel considering destination and current restrictions.

Do not assume every product can travel together. Batteries, liquids, magnetic goods, oversized products and other categories may have route-specific rules. The right question is not “Can I always consolidate?” but “Which live route accepts these exact contents for this destination?” Check the current LitBuy shipping information before submission.`],
    ['Why an early estimate is not a final charge', `An item’s CNY product price is separate from the cost to purchase, store, pack and transport the final parcel. A preliminary estimate can use incomplete dimensions or predicted weight. The final figure can change after warehouse handling, packing choices or route selection. Payment conversion, tax and possible import charges are separate considerations.

For a useful estimate, record the number of items, their categories, packaging sensitivity and destination, then compare only the live options shown for those details. A rate for a compact parcel is not a benchmark for footwear boxes or a mixed shipment. Do not rely on a generic rate as a personal quote.`],
    ['Parcel submission and tracking', `Before submitting a parcel, compare the inventory with what you intended to ship. Recheck the address, contact information, destination restrictions and selected services. Keep a copy of the route and order IDs. LitBuy’s public notices mention shipping information and tracking. Tracking can help follow a dispatched package, but it does not remove carrier delays, customs review, weather disruption or address problems.

When a status is unclear, use the current LitBuy support information and the carrier details shown for that shipment. Treat a tracking number as a status tool, not a delivery-date guarantee.`],
    ['Returns, restrictions and a final checklist', `LitBuy publishes refund and returns/exchange information, but an outcome is not automatic. Timing, seller terms, order stage, warehouse handling and current policy can matter. Shipping restrictions can also be set by the route, carrier, destination or law. A product appearing in a catalogue is not automatically eligible for every method. Read the applicable live policy before a parcel leaves China.

The practical checklist is: verify each selected item; review current warehouse/QC information; build an inventory with IDs and quantities; check live options for the destination and contents; read current restrictions and after-sales rules; submit only when address, parcel and route are correct; and keep order and tracking records until the shipment is resolved. This answers the keyword intent without inventing a fixed cost or time.`],
    ['Plan for a decision point rather than a promised date', `It is tempting to organise a parcel around a published transit estimate. A more reliable approach is to organise around decisions you control: when all items have arrived, when you have reviewed the available warehouse information, when you have checked current route restrictions and when the address and parcel contents are accurate. Carrier movement and import processing happen after those decisions and may not follow an expected calendar.

If the order is time-sensitive, read the current route information before purchase as well as before parcel submission. Build room into your own plan for changes in stock, warehouse arrival, route availability and transport. This does not make shipping slower; it prevents an estimate from being mistaken for a guarantee. The same restraint should be used when comparing community shipping stories, which may relate to a different date, destination, parcel or service.`]
  ]},
  { label: 'Article 04 · Product links', title: 'LitBuy Product Links: A Safer Way to Check Taobao, Weidian and 1688 Listings', description: 'How to turn a marketplace link into a clear research record before using an agent order flow.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: LitBuy product links · Related: LitBuy Taobao link, LitBuy Weidian link, LitBuy 1688 link, China shopping agent', sections: [
    ['Start with the live marketplace reference', `A product link is a pointer to a live listing, not a permanent product specification. LitBuy publicly explains that buyers can paste Taobao, Weidian and 1688 links into its search bar. That makes the link a useful starting point for an order request. It does not mean every option, image, price or inventory position shown earlier will still be available when the request is processed.

Before using a link, write down the item title, source ID, chosen option, displayed CNY amount and date checked. This small record helps distinguish the intended product from a similar listing later. If the source page changes, treat the current listing and current LitBuy order screen as the decision source.`],
    ['Choose the exact option before you submit', `Marketplace listings can contain deposits, accessories, colour variants, bundles and size choices under one URL. Read the option labels instead of relying on the cover photo. If the variation you want is not clear, do not infer it from a title. A specific question is safer than a vague instruction such as “same as picture.”

The internal catalogue can help you find a product ID or compare visible media, but it does not replace the live selection. Match the option name, quantity and price against the live page before you pay. This reduces the chance that a low listed amount belongs to an add-on or deposit rather than the full item.`],
    ['Keep discovery and checkout separate', `Use this site for internal discovery, category browsing and product comparison. When you decide to continue, open the final LitBuy route to inspect the current item and order terms. Keeping those jobs separate makes the process easier to audit: the catalogue explains what you researched; the live order flow confirms what is currently being requested.

Do not treat a copied link as proof of seller performance, authenticity, future stock or dispatch timing. Its value is simply that it identifies a listing you can check again. That is the honest answer to the search for LitBuy product links.`]
  ]},
  { label: 'Article 05 · Price guide', title: 'LitBuy Price Guide: Reading CNY Listings and USD Estimates Correctly', description: 'A practical guide to source prices, variants and why a browsing estimate is not a final total.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: LitBuy price · Related: LitBuy USD price, CNY conversion, China agent cost, product price estimate', sections: [
    ['Treat CNY as the source reference', `A product card may show an original CNY amount alongside a USD browsing estimate. The CNY amount is the marketplace reference captured from the source listing. The USD display helps compare records quickly, but it is an indicative conversion rather than a checkout quote. Currency movement and the conversion method can change an estimate.

The useful habit is to keep both figures visible in your notes. Compare catalogue records in one currency, then confirm the exact payment amount in the live LitBuy flow. This prevents an attractive converted number from being mistaken for a final bill.`],
    ['Understand what a listing price can omit', `The first visible amount on a marketplace page may belong to a deposit, a smaller variant, an accessory or a single part of a set. It may also exclude services and any future parcel costs. Open the live listing and compare each option before using a price as a benchmark.

Product price, agent charges, parcel shipping, payment conversion, tax and destination charges are separate questions. A clear price guide names those boundaries instead of combining them into an unsupported “total cost.”`],
    ['Compare products, not thumbnails', `A valid comparison matches the same version, size, colour and included pieces. Two near-identical images can lead to different products and different source prices. Retain the product ID and option name with each candidate so you can revisit the exact record later.

If a live price differs from the catalogue reference, use the live price for the current decision. Listings can change. A careful comparison is still valuable because it tells you what changed and whether you want to continue researching.`]
  ]},
  { label: 'Article 06 · Product categories', title: 'LitBuy Product Categories: How to Search Finds Without Losing the Original Listing', description: 'A category-first method for navigating shoes, clothing, accessories and other product records.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: LitBuy product categories · Related: LitBuy shoes, LitBuy clothing, LitBuy accessories, product finds', sections: [
    ['Use categories to narrow, not to certify', `Product categories make a large catalogue easier to browse. They describe how a visible source record has been grouped: shoes, accessories, jackets or another practical label. They do not certify a seller, confirm materials, establish size accuracy or guarantee that an item remains available.

Start in All Products when you know an ID or title; start in a category when you only know the kind of item. Keep the source title and ID beside every promising card because category labels are navigation tools, not permanent marketplace taxonomy.`],
    ['Search within a category with a clear question', `A focused query combines an item type with a visible feature, product ID or marketplace term. For example, a buyer might search a category for a particular colour, a number from a Weidian link or a product-name fragment. This is more reliable than scrolling until a familiar thumbnail appears.

When two records look similar, open both internal detail pages and compare the title, original CNY amount and visible images. Do not assume they are interchangeable merely because the category is the same.`],
    ['Build a shortlist that you can recheck', `Keep a shortlist short enough to verify. For each candidate, note category, source ID, visible title, option and date. Then use the live listing and LitBuy order flow to confirm the current details. A category page is most useful when it saves time without hiding uncertainty.

If the live product no longer matches your note, return to the category search rather than forcing the old choice. Discovery should make it easier to reconsider, not make an outdated record feel like a commitment.`]
  ]},
  { label: 'Article 07 · Size and variants', title: 'LitBuy Size and Variant Checks: What to Confirm Before You Place an Order', description: 'A research checklist for sizes, colours, bundles and configuration choices in marketplace listings.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: LitBuy size guide · Related: LitBuy size check, LitBuy colour option, product variant, China shopping size', sections: [
    ['Read the option names, not just the photo', `A product image can be shared by several sizes, colours and configurations. Before ordering, read the live variant label, any chart or visible specification, the quantity and the CNY amount attached to the option. This is especially important where one page mixes a full product with accessories or deposits.

An internal card can show a research reference, but it cannot select a variant for you. Keep the exact option text in your notes so there is no need to reconstruct a choice from memory later.`],
    ['Separate visible evidence from fit claims', `A chart or label may provide a measurement reference, yet it does not guarantee personal fit. Materials, cuts, production variation and measurement method can matter. For that reason, a responsible guide does not promise that a size will fit or that a colour will look identical under every light.

For an order-specific warehouse image, you may be able to check a visible label or colour. Use that as a check of the selected item, not as proof of fit, comfort or long-term quality.`],
    ['Make a precise order note', `Record the marketplace URL or ID, selected colour, selected size or configuration, quantity and the date checked. If a point is unclear, phrase the question exactly: ask about the named option or visible measurement rather than asking whether an item is “correct.”

Precision is useful at every stage. It makes it easier to compare a live order screen with your intended choice and easier to identify a visible mismatch before you make a later parcel decision.`]
  ]},
  { label: 'Article 08 · Parcel submission', title: 'LitBuy Parcel Submission: A Pre-Shipping Checklist for Your Warehouse Items', description: 'A clear preparation guide for shoppers researching LitBuy parcel submission and combined shipping.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: LitBuy parcel submission · Related: LitBuy warehouse items, LitBuy package, combined shipping, international parcel', sections: [
    ['Inventory before you submit', `A parcel submission starts with a simple inventory: product ID, visible description, quantity, order status and item type. Compare that list with the items you intend to send. If you have available inspection media, use it to review visible order-specific details before turning several orders into one parcel.

This preparation is more useful than relying on memory, especially with similar products or multiple orders. It also makes it easier to see whether an expected item is still absent or a question remains unresolved.`],
    ['Check current route eligibility', `Shipping options can depend on destination, line availability, contents, dimensions, actual or volumetric weight and current restrictions. Do not assume that products can travel together merely because they are stored together. Batteries, liquids, magnetic items, oversized goods and other categories can face route-specific limitations.

The live LitBuy parcel screen and current policy information are the place to check the exact destination and contents. A guide can prepare the questions; it cannot approve a parcel.`],
    ['Confirm address and records', `Before submitting, reread the address, contact information, selected route and parcel contents. Keep the order and route identifiers once a parcel is created. These are useful references if the carrier status changes or a support question is needed.

Submission is a decision point, not a delivery promise. Review current information before you proceed and leave time for warehouse handling, carrier events and import processes that are outside a catalogue’s control.`]
  ]},
  { label: 'Article 09 · Tracking guide', title: 'LitBuy Tracking: How to Read Parcel Status Without Assuming a Delivery Date', description: 'What a tracking number can help you monitor, and the limits of shipping-status updates.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: LitBuy tracking · Related: LitBuy package tracking, international parcel status, shipping update, China agent tracking', sections: [
    ['Keep identifiers from the start', `Order IDs, parcel IDs and any carrier tracking number serve different purposes. Keep them together with your destination and submission date. When a status is reviewed later, this record prevents one parcel from being confused with another.

Tracking is a status tool. It can indicate a carrier event or route milestone, but its wording and update frequency are controlled by the carrier and service. It should not be read as a guaranteed delivery appointment.`],
    ['Read status in context', `A delay can have many causes, including warehouse handling, carrier transfer, weather, holidays, route changes, customs processing or an address issue. A generic status cannot independently establish the cause. Check the current LitBuy order information and carrier details available for that exact shipment before drawing a conclusion.

Do not compare your parcel too closely with a community example that used another destination, date, carrier, item type or service. A tracking story is only useful when its conditions are genuinely comparable.`],
    ['Know when to ask a focused question', `If an update appears unclear, keep the parcel ID, tracking number, date and the exact status wording. Use the current official support route to ask about that record. A precise question is easier to investigate than a broad demand for a promised arrival date.

The practical goal of tracking is to maintain a documented view of a live shipment. It does not remove carrier or customs uncertainty, but it gives you the references needed to follow the process responsibly.`]
  ]},
  { label: 'Article 10 · Returns and after-sales', title: 'LitBuy Returns and After-Sales: Questions to Check Before International Shipping', description: 'A policy-first checklist for buyers researching refunds, exchanges and order questions.', keywords: 'Primary keyword: LitBuy return policy · Related: LitBuy refund, LitBuy exchange, after-sales, warehouse item question', sections: [
    ['Read the current policy for the current order', `Refund, return and exchange outcomes can depend on the product, seller, order stage, timing and current policy. LitBuy publishes after-sales and refund information, but a general article cannot determine an outcome for a particular item. Read the current official terms before relying on a remedy.

Avoid treating a catalogue listing or a generic online comment as an approval. The live order and official policy information are the appropriate places to understand what may be available.`],
    ['Document a visible question clearly', `If warehouse inspection media raises a concern, note the product ID, selected option, order reference, relevant image angle and the visible point you want clarified. Say what the image appears to show or does not show. Do not state conclusions about quality, authenticity or shipping that the image cannot prove.

This creates a useful factual record and keeps a product question distinct from a later parcel or carrier issue. It also makes it easier to consult the correct current policy.`],
    ['Pause before the irreversible stage', `International parcel submission can change the options available for an item. If there is an unresolved question, review the live order and policy information before submitting the parcel. Do not assume a refund or exchange is automatic after an item has moved to a later stage.

The responsible approach is simple: identify the exact issue, check the current terms, keep records and use the live support path for that order. It is more dependable than a broad promise that every problem will have the same solution.`]
  ]}
];

// Articles 01–03 already have seven or more editorial sections. These research
// extensions bring Articles 04–10 to the same long-form depth without adding
// claims beyond the live LitBuy order and policy information.
const longFormTopics = [
  null, null, null,
  { focus: 'a LitBuy product-link request', sources: 'Taobao, Weidian or 1688', decision: 'the exact listing, option and quantity you intend to request' },
  { focus: 'a CNY source-price comparison', sources: 'a marketplace listing and the live LitBuy order flow', decision: 'whether two visible offers actually describe the same product and option' },
  { focus: 'a category-based product search', sources: 'the internal category index and the original source record', decision: 'which small shortlist deserves a current live-listing check' },
  { focus: 'a size or configuration selection', sources: 'the live option label, visible chart and order-specific media', decision: 'whether the named variant matches your own intended specification' },
  { focus: 'a parcel-submission plan', sources: 'warehouse order records, the live parcel screen and current route information', decision: 'whether the exact contents can be submitted together for the destination' },
  { focus: 'a parcel-tracking update', sources: 'the exact parcel identifier, carrier status and current order information', decision: 'whether there is a specific, documented question to raise through the current support path' },
  { focus: 'an after-sales or return question', sources: 'the order record, visible inspection evidence and the current official policy', decision: 'which current policy question applies before a parcel is submitted' }
];

const longFormSections = (topic) => topic ? [
  ['Set a narrow research question first', `Long-form research works best when it begins with one concrete question, not a hope that a product page will answer everything. For ${topic.focus}, identify the one point that would change your next action. It may be an option name, a source price, a visible colour, a warehouse status or a route rule. Write that question next to the product ID and the date you checked it. This keeps a later comparison tied to an identifiable record instead of to an image remembered from a feed.

Start with ${topic.sources}. These can provide useful visible facts, but their role is different at each stage. A directory helps locate a record; a live listing helps confirm a current option; an order screen helps confirm what is being requested; and a policy page explains current conditions. None of those sources should be stretched into a promise about inventory, quality, authenticity, cost, customs treatment or delivery. The goal is a traceable next step, not a blanket verdict.`],
  ['Build a comparison note that can survive changes', `A short note is more useful than an unlabelled screenshot. Record the source ID or URL, title as shown, selected option, quantity, original CNY amount, date and the specific detail you were checking. For ${topic.focus}, add a one-sentence observation in plain language: what is visible, what is not visible, and which source you used. This makes a changed title or similar-looking replacement easier to spot later.

Do not fill gaps with assumptions. A missing angle is simply missing evidence; a low entry price may belong to a deposit or accessory; a category label is only a browsing aid. When two records are compared, verify that they describe the same variant and included pieces. A consistent note-taking method prevents a shopper from accidentally comparing a bundle with a single item, or a past source image with a current order selection.`],
  ['Use the live stage for the live decision', `The final decision for ${topic.decision} belongs at the live stage, not in an exported catalogue or an old community post. Listings, options, policy wording and available routes can change. Re-open the current details immediately before you act and compare them field by field with your note. If the item title, option, visible price or order information differs, pause and decide again from the current information.

This is also where restraint matters. An independent research page can explain a process and preserve a reference trail, but it cannot authenticate an item, approve a shipping method, forecast a carrier event or guarantee a remedy. The practical value is that it leaves you with a specific question and the records needed to ask it. That is much more useful than turning a product find into a claim that cannot be verified.`],
  ['Keep product, warehouse and parcel decisions separate', `Shoppers often combine several decisions into one: choosing a product, reviewing an arrived item and submitting an international parcel. They are separate decisions with different evidence. A source image may support product discovery. Order-specific inspection media may support a visible warehouse check. Parcel submission depends on the destination, actual contents, available services, dimensions and current restrictions. Approval at one stage is not automatic approval at the next.

