  'Turn the guide into a repeatable decision record',
  `The durable value of a guide such as “${article.title}” is not a one-time answer. It is a repeatable way to identify what you know, what you still need to check and which live screen is responsible for that answer. Begin with the FindSpreadsheet record and keep the source ID, title, category, CNY reference and date. When the task involves a specific option, add the option name, size, colour, configuration or quantity exactly as it appears in the current listing. When the task reaches a warehouse stage, add the order identifier and describe only the visible question raised by the available inspection media. When the task reaches a parcel stage, create a separate entry for the parcel identifier, destination, contents and current route information.

This record prevents evidence from being carried into the wrong decision. A visible product photo is not a shipping approval; a warehouse image is not an authenticity certificate; a tracking event is not a delivery promise; and an old price is not a current quotation. If a detail changes, write down the change rather than trying to reconcile it from memory. If information is missing, write “not confirmed” rather than guessing. The result is a calmer, more useful research path: compare the exported record, verify the live product or order information, read the active policy when it is relevant, and keep the exact identifiers needed for a focused support question. Review that record again immediately before payment or parcel submission, because the current live state is always more important than an earlier note. This is the standard used throughout this guide—specific evidence, clear limits and no invented outcome.`
