  {label:'Article 03 · Shipping and storage',title:'LitBuy Shipping and Storage: Plan a Parcel From an Inventory, Not a Guess',description:'A new article on storage, consolidation and the live checks needed before international shipment.',keywords:'Primary keyword: LitBuy shipping · Related: LitBuy storage, consolidation, parcel submission',content:`Shipping research becomes clearer when it is separated into three questions: what has arrived, what may be packed together, and which current route accepts the final parcel. LitBuy’s public homepage describes storage, inspection and combined shipping, while its help materials publish storage information and shipping guidance. Those pages explain the process, not a universal quote. Storage allowances, route availability and fees can change, so use the live account, order screen and current policy as the source for a particular item.

Start with an inventory rather than a pile of order numbers. Record each item’s order ID, product ID, title, quantity, category, warehouse status and any open question from inspection. This gives you a reliable way to tell whether the intended parcel is complete. It also prevents an easy error: submitting a package because similar-looking items have arrived while one selected order is still missing or unresolved.

Consolidation is a planning option, not a blanket eligibility rule. The destination, selected line, actual or volumetric weight, dimensions and contents can all affect what is available. Some item types may have restrictions that differ by route. Check the live parcel interface for the exact destination and contents rather than copying an old estimate or a community example from another country. The source-item price, agent costs, packing choices, shipping charge, exchange conversion, taxes and possible import charges are separate parts of the overall cost.

