The final category lesson is restraint. A category label is not a product verdict. Treat the category page as a discovery map, the live marketplace listing as the place to check current options, and the order screen as the place to verify a request. This sequence is less dramatic than calling a product a guaranteed find, but it gives the reader a reliable route from broad search to a checkable product decision.`},
  {label:'Article 07 · Size and variants',title:'LitBuy Size and Variant Checks: What to Confirm Before You Order',description:'A new guide to reducing mistakes with colour, size, bundles and configuration choices.',keywords:'Primary keyword: LitBuy size guide · Related: LitBuy size check, product variant, colour option',content:`Variant mistakes usually happen because a cover image feels more certain than an option label. A single marketplace page can contain several colours, sizes, deposits, accessories and configurations. Before you proceed, read the exact live option name, the amount attached to that option, quantity and any visible size chart or specification. Copy the option text into your own note. “Same as picture” is not a useful instruction when a page has several visually similar choices.

